 in  the game you know after the flagrant  foul and now he you know he doesn't  suffer the penalty of not playing  subsequent into that and still is going  to play in game 4 just seems a little  bit unfair how involved is the  Commissioner in these decisions I know  you made these decisions for a long time  how involved is the Commissioner it was  David Stern with you obvious  silver now well to be clear you know and  uh I my job and kikis job is to make  recommendations to the commissioner  ultimately he is the key decision-maker  and he's the key decision-maker why  because these things matter fans care  about them you know a decision one way  or the other can affect the NBA bran or  affect you know the perception of  integrity in the game it's a pretty big  deal and the Commissioner should be  making those decisions and you know when  I was a part of the NBA I made those  recommendations to David Stern and yes  more times than not he accepted my you  know recommendation but he has the right  and it's his prerogative to say no you  know Stu or Adam in the case of kiki no  that's not the way we're going to go so  at the end of the day you don't know you  know what Kiki Vandeweghe recommendation  is and and and but ultimately that  decision rests with the Commissioner  when you looks to what happened with  Dante Jones and now what happened with  draymond Green is it fair to say that  there's a a different standard for star  players in the NBA well I mean to be  clear the doctor Jones you know was a  pretty bright line rule in that you know  he basically took a little flick or a  little swing and hit beyond bow in the  groin and the rule clearly says if you  do that whether you have a closed fist  or and opens this contact or not because  you're going to suffer an automatic  one-game suspension so in that regard  it's different but clearly if you look  at you know the way that physical  contact has been evaluated throughout  these playoffs you know it leads you to  a notion that there is a little bit of a  double standard and case in point you  know Durant flagrant foul penalty to  where he took justin anderson Ted off  Isiah Thomas where he swung and he hit  Schroeder in the head you've got now the  you know the draymond Green situation  there was another non call where another  star player hits another star player  Andre Drummond hits you know LeBron  James in the head clearly the standard  is in playoff games they want to keep  these better players in the game  and you know not not suspend them for  the next one which is again okay that's  fine but then don't have the rule  written that calls for an automatic  suspension in these cases cases rewrite  the rule and let everybody know what  that rule is so the NBA is not in a  situation where they leave everything to  chance and leaves people wondering  exactly where the line is so here's what  I understand those two because you just  said if you look at what's happened so  far this postseason it would lead you  believe there is a double standard for  star players still you did this right i  mean you were the one that made these  decisions with the commissioner for a  while so you would know whether or not  there's a double standard right i mean  what what what took place when you were  the one doing it well listen you know  what took place is is that we tried to  be very consistent treat all players  equally because we felt it was important  to the integrity of the game and that  was never more exemplified and listen  you can debate this and people will for  maybe perhaps another decade what we did  in 2007 in the San Antonio Phoenix Sun  series were resuspended Boris BL and  Amar'e Stoudemire for leaving the  vicinity of the bench during an  altercation where they left 20 you know  they were 25 feet away from the bench so  listen that was a very difficult  decision it was painful but the decision  was made in the interest of consistency  and integrity because there's a rule o